\section{Extended SNR Analysis}
\label{app:snr_extended}

We measure per-layer gradient SNR at 14 checkpoints across the full 1000-step training trajectory (LLaMA-3.1-8B, C4) with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals.
BCE maintains consistently higher SNR than KL throughout training: $5.0\times$ at step 300 ($1.742{\times}10^{-3}$ vs.\ $3.47{\times}10^{-4}$) and $2.3\times$ at convergence (step 1000: $2.026{\times}10^{-3}$ vs.\ $8.78{\times}10^{-4}$). % exp_id: snr_full_curve_1000steps
Both trajectories increase monotonically, reflecting predictor convergence, with no crossover at any point.
KL's noisier gradients nevertheless produce superior masks (Table~\ref{tab:main}): this confirms that the decisive factor is \emph{target alignment}---optimizing toward the output distribution---rather than gradient clarity.
BCE's higher SNR reflects its simpler per-neuron classification objective, which concentrates gradients on individual neuron decisions; KL's coupled gradient distributes signal across all mask decisions jointly, yielding lower SNR but discovering compositionally superior mask patterns (\S\ref{sec:method:gradient}).


\section{Extended Downstream Evaluation}
\label{app:downstream}

Table~\ref{tab:downstream} reports HellaSwag and PIQA across sparsity levels and allocation strategies.

